  "body": {
    "id": "prop:full-bridge-recovery",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "In any fixed stratum of the rank-one design submodel, generic recovery of the full \\(W\\)-indexed bridge holds if and only if \\(q_x=d\\) and \\(\\operatorname{TreePack}_d(G)\\) holds. If \\(q_x<d\\), no addition of randomized comparison edges can recover the full bridge in that stratum, although selected target contrasts may still satisfy \\(\\ker(A_P)\\subseteq\\ker(R_0)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Wcal",
      "g",
      "q_x",
      "d",
      "treepack_q",
      "G",
      "A_Px",
      "A_P",
      "R_0",
      "Xcal"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:exact-nullity",
      "thm:proxy-rank-ceiling",
      "thm:generic-tree-rank"
    ],
    "justification": "The corollary separates full nuisance recovery from identification of the causal functional.",
    "gap": "Finite-proxy rank results do not distinguish graph saturation of effective coordinates from full bridge recovery in a trial network.",
    "consumer": "NETMOTION analysts can see why additional trials may identify clinically relevant contrasts without ever identifying every proxy-indexed bridge coordinate.",
    "proof_tex": "In one stratum the bridge has \\(d(|V|-1)\\) coordinates, so recovery of the full \\(W\\)-indexed bridge is equivalent to \\(\\ker(A_{P,x})=\\{0\\}\\), or \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A_{P,x})=d(|V|-1)\\).  The proxy-rank ceiling gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A_{P,x})\\le q_x(|V|-1)\\le d(|V|-1).\n\\]\nConsequently full recovery requires \\(q_x=d\\).  If \\(q_x=d\\), the generic-tree-rank theorem says that generic rank equals \\(d(|V|-1)\\) exactly when \\(\\operatorname{TreePack}_d(G)\\) holds.  This proves the equivalence.  If \\(q_x<d\\), the ceiling remains strict regardless of how many comparison edges are added, because adding edges does not change the fixed common-kernel rank.  On the other hand, exact target identification only requires \\(R_0\\ker(A_P)=\\{0\\}\\), which can hold with a nonzero bridge nullspace, as asserted.",
